<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rupa Goswami</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1489–1564</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rupa Goswami was a theologian and poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism">Gaudiya Vaishnavism</a>, one of the six Goswamis of Vrindavan. With his brother Sanatana he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> as a high official before leaving to follow Chaitanya, who sent him to Vrindavan. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works, especially the Bhakti-rasamrita-sindhu, set out the theory of devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> for the movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rupa-goswami.jpg" alt="Rupa Goswami" width="282" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HareKrishnaPhotos · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rupa_Goswami.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
